::ENG4_U01_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Before changing the schedule for a public study center, the planning team gathered feedback from students, tutors, and local employers. What does stakeholder mean? {
=A. A person or group affected by a decision#Correct. Students, tutors, and employers are stakeholders.
~B. A sentence fragment#This is a convention term, not stakeholder.
~C. A complete agreement#This is consensus.
~D. A source quotation#This is not stakeholder.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The comments did not produce complete consensus, but they revealed a shared concern: evening access mattered for students who worked during the day. Which sentence uses consensus accurately? {
~A. The group reached consensus because no one agreed.#This contradicts the meaning.
=B. The group lacked complete consensus, but several people shared one concern.#Correct. This matches the passage.
~C. Consensus means one person made a decision alone.#This is incorrect.
~D. Consensus means attendance data after three months.#This is incorrect.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The team recommended extending hours twice a week and reviewing attendance data after three months. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. The team recommended extending hours every night permanently.#This overstates the source.
~B. The team recommended closing the study center earlier.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. The team proposed a limited extension of hours with later attendance review.#Correct. This preserves the recommendation.
~D. The team refused to review attendance data.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. What does credible mean? {
~A. Unclear#This is the opposite.
~B. Unrelated#This is not credible.
~C. Informal#This is unrelated.
=D. Trustworthy#Correct. The recommendation is believable because it has reasons and a review plan.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. Which oral explanation is best organized? {
~A. It was credible, and there were people, and things happened.#This is vague.
=B. The recommendation is credible because it responds to stakeholders, uses a trial period, and includes an evaluation plan.#Correct. This is coherent and focused.
~C. The recommendation was consensus because it was feedback.#This misuses terms.
~D. The schedule changed, so everyone agreed.#This overstates the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The comments did not produce complete consensus, but they revealed a shared concern. What does shared concern mean? {
~A. A permanent schedule change#This is not a concern.
~B. A spelling correction#This is unrelated.
~C. A complete lack of feedback#This contradicts the passage.
=D. An issue more than one stakeholder cared about#Correct. Several groups were concerned about evening access.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The comments did not produce complete consensus, but they revealed a shared concern: evening access mattered for students who worked during the day. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Everyone fully agreed on every detail.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Students did not care about evening access.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. Although full agreement was missing, feedback showed common concern about evening access.#Correct. This preserves the limitation and shared concern.
~D. Employers refused to provide feedback.#This is unsupported.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Before changing the schedule for a public study center, the planning team gathered feedback from students, tutors, and local employers. Which opening is strongest for a brief oral explanation? {
=A. The planning team made a credible recommendation by gathering feedback from key stakeholders.#Correct. This previews claim and evidence clearly.
~B. This passage has some people in it and a schedule.#This is vague.
~C. I think study centers are nice.#This is personal opinion and lacks evidence.
~D. Feedback is a word that appears in the passage.#This identifies a word without reasoning.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The team recommended extending hours twice a week and reviewing attendance data after three months. Which word best describes "extending hours twice a week"? {
~A. Stakeholder#This names people, not action.
=B. Recommendation#Correct. It is the suggested action.
~C. Consensus#This means agreement.
~D. Rationale#This means reason.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. What does evaluating mean? {
~A. Ignoring#This is the opposite.
~B. Decorating#This is unrelated.
~C. Guessing without evidence#Evaluation uses evidence.
=D. Judging or assessing#Correct. Evaluating results means assessing them.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. Which paraphrase is strongest? {
~A. The recommendation was good for no clear reason.#This misses the rationale.
~B. The recommendation was credible because everyone agreed completely.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. The recommendation was trustworthy because it addressed concerns, tested the change, and planned to review data.#Correct. This preserves the rationale.
~D. The recommendation was credible because data would never be reviewed.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The team recommended extending hours twice a week and reviewing attendance data after three months. Which sentence is best for an oral audience? {
~A. The recommendation, by virtue of temporal extension, manifests procedural review.#This is inflated and hard to follow.
=B. The team suggested later hours twice a week and planned to check attendance after three months.#Correct. This is clear and accurate for oral delivery.
~C. The team did stuff later and data things happened.#This is vague.
~D. The schedule was a recommendation stakeholder consensus.#This misuses vocabulary.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. Which sentence uses rationale correctly? {
~A. The rationale is the people affected by the decision.#That defines stakeholders.
~B. The rationale means everyone agreed completely.#That is consensus.
~C. The rationale is the schedule itself.#That is the recommendation, not the reason.
=D. The rationale explains why the recommendation is reasonable.#Correct. Rationale means reason.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The comments did not produce complete consensus, but they revealed a shared concern: evening access mattered for students who worked during the day. Which statement distorts the passage? {
~A. The comments showed a shared concern about evening access.#This is accurate.
~B. Evening access mattered for students who worked during the day.#This is accurate.
=C. The comments produced complete agreement from every group.#Correct. The passage says complete consensus did not occur.
~D. The feedback did not lead to complete consensus.#This is accurate.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. Which explanation uses a rhetorical structure most effectively? {
=A. First, it responds to stakeholder concerns; second, it tests the change; finally, it evaluates results.#Correct. The structure guides the listener.
~B. It is credible, credible, credible because credible.#This is repetitive and circular.
~C. The plan is good because I like longer hours.#This relies on personal preference.
~D. Stakeholders and consensus and rationale and recommendation.#This is a list, not an explanation.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The comments did not produce complete consensus, but they revealed a shared concern. Which word signals limitation? {
~A. complete#This describes consensus but does not signal contrast.
~B. shared#This describes the concern.
=C. but#Correct. But signals contrast or limitation.
~D. concern#This names the issue.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Before changing the schedule for a public study center, the planning team gathered feedback from students, tutors, and local employers. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. The team changed the schedule without asking anyone.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. The team gathered feedback only from employers.#This is too narrow.
~C. The team ignored students and tutors.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. The team collected input from groups affected by the study center schedule before making a change.#Correct. This preserves stakeholder feedback.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. What is a trial period? {
~A. A permanent decision with no review#This is the opposite.
=B. A temporary test period#Correct. A trial period tests a change.
~C. A stakeholder group#This is not a time period.
~D. A complete consensus#This is agreement.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The team recommended extending hours twice a week and reviewing attendance data after three months. Which spoken version is most coherent? {
=A. The recommendation was to extend hours twice a week, then review attendance data after three months.#Correct. It is clear and sequenced.
~B. Hours, data, months, twice, recommendation.#This is not coherent.
~C. The team maybe changed stuff because schedules.#This is vague.
~D. Attendance data is a phrase and the study center exists.#This does not explain.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Before changing the schedule for a public study center, the planning team gathered feedback from students, tutors, and local employers. Which word best describes students, tutors, and local employers together? {
~A. Rationale#This means reason.
~B. Trial period#This is a temporary test.
=C. Stakeholders#Correct. They are affected groups.
~D. Recommendation#This is suggested action.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. Which detail is part of the rationale for credibility? {
=A. The plan included a way to evaluate results.#Correct. Evaluation supports credibility.
~B. The team ignored feedback.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. The schedule changed permanently immediately.#This overstates the passage.
~D. Complete consensus was reached.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. Which sentence best maintains a coherent focus? {
~A. The recommendation is credible, and study centers have furniture.#This adds unrelated detail.
~B. The recommendation is about time, and time is important in life.#This drifts away from the passage.
~C. Stakeholders are people, and people talk, and schedules exist.#This lacks focus.
=D. The recommendation is credible because each part of the plan responds to concerns while allowing results to be checked.#Correct. This stays focused.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The comments did not produce complete consensus, but they revealed a shared concern. Which sentence uses concern accurately? {
=A. A concern is an issue people care about or worry about.#Correct. This matches the passage.
~B. A concern is a final attendance report.#This is incorrect.
~C. A concern means complete agreement.#This is consensus.
~D. A concern means the recommended action.#This is recommendation.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: This recommendation was credible because it responded to stakeholder concerns, used a limited trial period, and included a plan for evaluating results. Which paraphrase is too vague? {
~A. The recommendation was credible because it addressed concerns, tested a limited change, and planned for evaluation.#This is accurate.
=B. It was good because it had stuff.#Correct. This is too vague for Grade 12 work.
~C. The plan had reasons that made it trustworthy.#This is general but still meaningful.
~D. The plan was credible because it included feedback, a trial, and evaluation.#This is accurate.
}
::ENG4_U01_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Before changing the schedule for a public study center, the planning team gathered feedback from students, tutors, and local employers. The team recommended extending hours twice a week and reviewing attendance data after three months. Which explanation best combines academic vocabulary and oral clarity? {
=A. The recommendation is credible because it responds to stakeholder feedback and includes a plan to evaluate attendance data after a limited trial period.#Correct. The sentence is precise and coherent.
~B. The schedule thing is good because people said stuff.#This is vague and informal.
~C. Consensus stakeholders recommended rationale because data.#This misuses academic vocabulary.
~D. The recommendation is definitely perfect because everyone fully agreed.#This overstates the passage.
}
